We begin with some useful definitions for this proof. The error term $\epsilon$ defined to be the maximum amount any cost is perturbed: $\epsilon = \dist{c}{c'}{\infty}$. We use $u^{(t)}_i(\alpha)$ to denote the utility of a type $t \in [T]$ agent choosing action $i \in [n]$ under a linear contract of transfer coefficient $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ in the original principal-agent problem $\left(\bm{c}, \bm{F}, \bm{r}\right)$; we use $u'^{(t)}_i(\alpha)$ to denote the same for the perturbed principal-agent problem $(\bm{c}', \bm{F}, \bm{r})$. We remove the $i$ subscripts to denote the max over all actions; $u^{(t)}(\alpha)$ denotes the maximum utility our type $t$ agent can achieve under a linear contract of transfer coefficient $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ in the original problem $\left(\bm{c}, \bm{F}, \bm{r}\right)$ and $u'^{(t)}(\alpha)$ denotes the same for the perturbed problem $(\bm{c}', \bm{F}, \bm{r})$. Formally, we can write these four functions as:
\begin{align*}
  u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &= \alpha R^{(t)}_i - c_i \\
  u'^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &= \alpha R^{(t)}_i - c'_i = u_i(\alpha) \pm \epsilon \\
  u^{(t)}(\alpha) &= \max_i u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) \\
  u'^{(t)}(\alpha) &= \max_i u'^{(t)}_i (\alpha) = u^{(t)}(\alpha) \pm \epsilon
\end{align*}

In the original principal-agent problem $\left(\bm{c}, \bm{F}, \bm{r}\right)$, the principal could convince a type $t$ agent to choose action $i \in [n]$ if said action was utility-maximizing, i.e. $u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) = u^{(t)}(\alpha)$. Imagine that we fix a particular mapping from types to actions $(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)$, signifying that we want type $t$ to choose action $i_t$. As we vary our choice of $\alpha$, this mapping is either (i) can never be chosen or (ii) can be chosen when $\alpha$ is in some closed subset of $[0, 1]$. We will denote the set of $(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)$ that fall into the former class as $I_1$ and the set that fall into the latter class as $I_2$. The proof plan is that when the principal adjusts all costs (effectively adjusting all utilities) by $\epsilon$, the region of $\alpha$ that produce a specific class (i) mapping remains empty while the region that produces a specific class (ii) mapping does not grow much larger.

\textbf{Case 1.} The mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T) \in I_1$ is currently infeasible for all $\alpha \in [0, 1]$, and we need ensure that we preserve this property when we choose our perturbation threshold $\tau$. To do so, we examine the largest amount any action $i^*_t$ lags behind the best action: $\max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right]$; we know that for all $\alpha \in [0, 1]$, this expression is strictly positive. We will choose our perturbation threshold $\tau$ to guarantee that the most lagging action cannot catch up:
\begin{align*}
  \tau &\triangleq \frac13 \left[ \min_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_1, \alpha \in [0, 1]} \max_{t \in T} u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i_t}(\alpha) \right] > 0
\end{align*}

We now need to prove that this choice was good enough to get our desired guarantee. Consider the perturbed problem $(\bm{c}', \bm{F}, \bm{r})$ where the analogous lag expression is $u'^{(t)}(\alpha) - u'^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)$. Observe that for all $\alpha \in [0, 1]$:
\begin{align*}
  &\phantom{=} \max_{t \in T} \left[ u'^{(t)}(\alpha) - u'^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right] \\
  &= \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right] \pm 2 \epsilon \\
  &\ge \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right] - 2 \tau \\
  &\ge \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right]
       - \frac23 \left[ \min_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_1, \tilde{\alpha} \in [0, 1]}
                        \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\tilde{\alpha}) - u^{(t)}_{i_t}(\tilde{\alpha}) \right] \right] \\
  &\ge \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right] - \frac23 \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*}(\alpha) \right] \\
  &\ge \frac13 \max_{t \in T} \left[ u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \right] \\
  &> 0
\end{align*}
Hence we have successfully guaranteed that the mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T) \in I_1$ remains infeasible via our choice of perturbation threshold $\tau$.

\textbf{Case 2.} The mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T) \in I_2$ is currently feasible, and we want to control the growth of its feasible region. If we are indeed committed to forcing the mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)$, then we would always prefer a smaller sharing coefficient $\alpha$ (since that maximizes resulting profit). In other words, we just need to control how much the smallest feasible $\alpha$ decreases. Suppose that in the original problem $\left(\bm{c}, \bm{F}, \bm{r}\right)$ the smallest feasible value was $\alpha^*$. Formally, we know that:
\begin{align*}
  u^{(t)}(\alpha^*) &= u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha^*) \\
  \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \quad
  u^{(t)}(\alpha) &> u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \\
  \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \quad
  \exists i \in [n] \quad
  u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &> u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
\end{align*}

But our agent's utility for any action is linear in $\alpha$. In other words, the functions $u^{(t)}_i(\alpha)$ and $u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)$ can only cross once, and some particular action $i \in [n]$ witnesses the optimality of $\alpha^*$.
\begin{align}
  \label{ineq:original}
  \exists i \in [n] \quad
  \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \quad
  u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &> u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
\end{align}

To determine how much the smallest feasible $\alpha$ could decrease in the perturbed problem, we recall the need for our desired actions to not lag utility-wise (i.e. be tied for the best utility). In other words, we know that:
\begin{align}
  \nonumber
  u'^{(t)}(\alpha) &=
  u'^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \\
  \nonumber
  \implies \forall i \in [n] \quad
  u'^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &\le
  u'^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) \\
  \label{ineq:perturbed}
  \implies \forall i \in [n] \quad
  u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) - 2 \epsilon &\le
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
\end{align}
Inequality~\ref{ineq:perturbed} will help us control how much lower $\alpha$ can be set in the perturbed problem, and hence how much additional profit the principal can extract there. We can instantiate it for any action $i \in [n]$, and we will choose the action defined by Inequality~\ref{ineq:original}.

We want to deduce a small fact about our action $i$, so we split into two subcases, depending on the relationship between the expected principal rewards $R^{(t)}_{i^*_t}$ (expected reward when type $t$ plays desired action $i^*_t$) and $R^{(t)}_{i}$ (expected reward when type $t$ plays alternate action $i$).

\textbf{Subcase 2(a).} Suppose our type-action pair satisfies $R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} \le R^{(t)}_i$, so we can deduce:
\begin{align*}
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha^*)          &\ge u^{(t)}_i(\alpha^*) & \\
  \alpha^* R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - c_{i^*} &\ge \alpha^* R^{(t)}_i - c_i & \\
  \alpha   R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - c_{i^*} &\ge \alpha   R^{(t)}_i - c_i & \forall \alpha \le \alpha^* \\
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)            &\ge u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) & \forall \alpha \le \alpha^*
\end{align*}
We have shown such an alternate action $i$ cannot be the action guaranteed by Inequality~\ref{ineq:original}. Hence that action must fall into the subcase below instead.

\textbf{Subcase 2(b).} This is the only subcase left, so we have deduced that the action $i$ guaranteed by Inequality~\ref{ineq:original} satisfies $R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} > R^{(t)}_i$. Combining what we know yields:
\begin{align*}
\begin{array}{rll}
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
    &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
    &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) + \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \left(R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i\right) - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} R^{(t)}_{i^*_t}
    &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} R^{(t)}_i - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}\left( \alpha - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \right)
    &< u^{(t)}_i\left( \alpha  - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \right) - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
  u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)& < u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) - 2 \epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* - \frac{2 \epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i}
\end{array}
\end{align*}

We have proven that the smallest feasible $\alpha$ may decrease by at most $\frac{2 \epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i}$ (note: there was also an unhandled case where $\alpha^*$ was already zero, but that leads to this conclusion as well). This increases the potential profit of $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)$ by at most $\frac{2 \epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \sum_{\tilde{t}=1}^T R^{(t)}_{i^*_t}$. We can cover this by choosing our error scaling term $s$ to be:
\begin{align*}
  s &\triangleq \max_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_2, t \in [T], i \in [n] . R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} > R^{(t)}_i} \frac{2 \sum_{\tilde{t}} R^{(\tilde{t})}_{i^*_t}}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i}
\end{align*}
(or zero, if for all Case 2 mappings $\alpha^* = 0$). This completes the proof.

\begin{align*}
    u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &= \alpha R^{(t)}_i - c_i \\
    u'^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &= \alpha R^{(t)}_i - c'_i = u_i(\alpha) \pm \epsilon \\
    u^{(t)}(\alpha) &= \max_i u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) \\
    u'^{(t)}(\alpha) &= \max_i u'^{(t)}_i (\alpha) = u^{(t)}(\alpha) \pm \epsilon
  \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
    \tau &\triangleq \frac13 \left[ \min_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_1, \alpha \in [0, 1]} \max_{t \in T} u^{(t)}(\alpha) - u^{(t)}_{i_t}(\alpha) \right] > 0
  \end{align*}

\begin{align}
    \label{ineq:original}
    \exists i \in [n] \quad
    \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \quad
    u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) &> u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
  \end{align}

\begin{align*}
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha^*)          &\ge u^{(t)}_i(\alpha^*) & \\
    \alpha^* R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - c_{i^*} &\ge \alpha^* R^{(t)}_i - c_i & \\
    \alpha   R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - c_{i^*} &\ge \alpha   R^{(t)}_i - c_i & \forall \alpha \le \alpha^* \\
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)            &\ge u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) & \forall \alpha \le \alpha^*
  \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
  \begin{array}{rll}
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
      &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)
      &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) + \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \left(R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i\right) - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha) - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} R^{(t)}_{i^*_t}
      &< u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} R^{(t)}_i - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}\left( \alpha - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \right)
      &< u^{(t)}_i\left( \alpha  - \frac{2\epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i} \right) - 2\epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* \\
    u^{(t)}_{i^*_t}(\alpha)& < u^{(t)}_i(\alpha) - 2 \epsilon & \forall \alpha < \alpha^* - \frac{2 \epsilon}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i}
  \end{array}
  \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
    s &\triangleq \max_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_2, t \in [T], i \in [n] . R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} > R^{(t)}_i} \frac{2 \sum_{\tilde{t}} R^{(\tilde{t})}_{i^*_t}}{R^{(t)}_{i^*_t} - R^{(t)}_i}
  \end{align*}

As in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:linear-stable}, we define the error term $\epsilon = \dist{c}{c'}{\infty}$. This proof proceeds in a similar fashion, except that our arguments are now based on linear programs and we are less precise in choosing $s$ and $\tau$.

In the original principal-agent problem $\left(\bm{c}, \bm{F}, \bm{r}\right)$, the principal could get the mapping $(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)$ with minimum expected transfers by solving the following linear program:
\begin{align*}
LP_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)} \left\{
  \begin{array}{rll}
                \min_x & \sum_{t=1}^T \sum_{j=1}^m F^{(t)}_{i_t j} x_j \\
    \\
    \text{subject to } & \sum_{j=1}^m \left[ F^{(t)}_{i_tj} - F^{(t)}_{ij} \right] x_j \ge c_{i_t} - c_i & \forall t \in [T], i \in [n] \\
                       & x_j \ge 0                                                                       & \forall j \in [m]
  \end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}
Let $I_1$ be the set of mappings $(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)$ whose linear program $LP_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)}$ is infeasible; $I_2$, feasible.

\textbf{Case 1.} The mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)$ currently has an infeasible LP and we want to maintain this status quo. We will choose our perturbation threshold $\tau$ to keep it so. We can determine how far this mapping is from feasible (again, we pretend the principal can pick a different perturbation for each type, giving it more power) by writing another linear program:
\begin{align*}
LP'_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)} \left\{
  \begin{array}{rll}
    \min_{x, \epsilon} & \epsilon \\ 
    \\
    \text{subject to } & \sum_{j=1}^m \left[ F^{(t)}_{i_tj} - F^{(t)}_{ij} \right] x_j + 2 \epsilon \ge c_{i_t} - c_i & \forall t \in [T], i \in [n] \\
                       & x_j \ge 0                                                                                    & \forall j \in [m]
  \end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}
Since $LP_{(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)}$ was infeasible, we know that $LP'_{(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)}$ has a strictly positive optimal value (the latter is feasible because very large perturbations e.g. larger than all costs suffice). We hence can choose our perturbation threshold $\tau$ to be:
\begin{align*}
  \tau \triangleq \frac12 \min_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_1} \text{value}(LP'_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)})
\end{align*}

\textbf{Case 2.} The mapping $(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)$ currently has a feasible LP and we want to control how much additional profit the principal can extract from it. Consider the following dual to $LP_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)}$:
\begin{align*}
D_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)} \left\{
  \begin{array}{rll}
                \max_y & \sum_{t=1}^T \sum_{i=1}^n (c_{i_t} - c_i) y^{(t)}_i \\
    \\
    \text{subject to } & \sum_{t=1}^T \sum_{i=1}^n \left[ F^{(t)}_{i_tj} - F^{(t)}_{ij} \right] y_i \le \sum_{t=1}^T F^{(t)}_{i_tj} & \forall j \in [m] \\
                       & y^{(t)}_i \ge 0                                                                                            & \forall t \in [T], i \in [n]
  \end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}

By strong LP-duality, we know that $LP_{(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)}$ and $D_{(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)}$ have the same optimal value. Additionally, due to \cite{grotschel2012geometric} there exists a dual point $y^*$ which achieves this optimal value whose $\ell_\infty$ norm is exponential in the bit-complexity of $D_{(i^*_1, i^*_2, \ldots, i^*_T)}$. Now, suppose that the costs $c$ get perturbed by $\epsilon$ into $c'$. Our previous dual solution $y^*$ is still feasible, because only the dual objective gets changed. In fact, its dual objective value drops by at most the $\ell_\infty$ norm of $y^*$. Hence even under perturbed costs $c'$ the optimal value of the dual (and primal, by strong LP-duality) also drops by that amount. Profit goes up by at most the same amount, and hence it suffices to choose our error scaling term $s$ to be:
\begin{align*}
  s \triangleq \max_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T) \in I_2} 2^{1 + O(\text{bit-complexity}(D_{(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_T)})}
\end{align*}

This completes the proof.

